 ],
 "literal": "Like an elephant washed so that dirt is removed, / on the dark, rough boulder that endured heavy rain, / the lord of the land where green moss rests on one side / gave me this affliction, O friend! / Pallor has filled my beautiful blue-lily-like eyes.",
 "translation": "Like an elephant washed clean of all dirt, a dark, rough boulder has weathered the heavy rain, and green moss now clings to one side of it. The lord of the land where such boulders lie has brought this painful affliction upon me, my friend, and my eyes, once like beautiful blue water-lilies, are now filled with the pale yellow of grief.",
 "modern": "தோழியே! அழுக்கு நீங்குமாறு கழுவப்பட்ட யானையைப் போல, பெருமழையைத் தாங்கிய கரிய சொரசொரப்பான பாறையின் ஒரு பக்கத்தில் பசுமையான பாசி படர்ந்திருக்கும் மலைநாட்டைச் சேர்ந்த தலைவன் எனக்குத் துன்பத்தைத் தந்துவிட்டான். அதனால், குவளை மலர் போன்ற என் கண்கள் பசலை நிறம் அடைந்துவிட்டன.",
 "explanation": "In this Kuriñci poem, the heroine is speaking to her close friend about her distress during the hero's absence. She describes the hero's mountainous land, where heavy rains wash the dark, rough boulders until they resemble clean elephants, leaving green moss to grow on one side. This vivid natural image serves as an ulluṟai (implied metaphor): just as the dark boulder is weathered by the rain and gets covered in moss, the heroine's dark, beautiful eyes have weathered the rain of her tears and are now covered by the pale yellow-green tint of payalai (pallor of separation). The key words are 'tuṟukal' (boulder), 'paital' (green moss/distress), 'nōy' (affliction), and 'payalai' (pallor). Through this comparison, she conveys how the hero's absence has physically transformed her beauty into a state of grief.",
